            <ArticleTitle>Estimated GFR or albuminuria: which one is really associated with resistant hypertension?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>492-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.semnephrol.2014.08.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0270-9295(14)00107-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Patients with resistant hypertension belong to a very high cardiovascular risk group and have a high prevalence of target organ damage. Microalbuminuria and low estimated glomerular filtration rate are associated with resistant hypertension, and could be a cause and/or complication of hypertension. In this review, we explore the relationship between these 2 markers of kidney disease and the prevalence of resistant hypertension. We identified different phenotypes of resistant hypertension that associate with microalbuminuria and/or low estimated glomerular filtration rate. These phenotypes suggest that high sympathetic activity associated with fluid overload and endothelial dysfunction may contribute differently to the development of resistant hypertension.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
